WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.540
[chirp]

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.240
That sound signals two black holes colliding in space.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.540
Not as loud as you'd expect for a monster merger,

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:12.780
but back here on earth,

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.080
that little chirp represents something revolutionary --

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.800
the first direct evidence for gravitational waves.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.800
Two giant detectors that make up the Advanced

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.460
Laser Interferometer Gravitational Wave Observatory

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:30.360
(or LIGO for short)

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.600
picked up waves from the cataclysmic collision of

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.780
two black holes about a billion light-years away.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.940
The discovery opens up a new window on the cosmos

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.540
much like the invention of the telescope did.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:45.580
A century ago, Albert Einstein predicted that big things,

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:46.860
like planets,

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.860
bend the fabric of spacetime.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:51.760
In Einstein's universe, gravity isn't a force --

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.140
at least not in the way we typically think about forces.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.700
It's just what happens when stars, planets and moons

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.480
follow those curves.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.420
When an object --- any object -- accelerates,

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.120
it produces ripples in the fabric of spacetime,

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.400
known as gravitational waves.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.160
These energetic ripples radiate out

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.680
and compress and stretch that fabric

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.420
similar to how sound waves vibrate the air.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.040
You produced some gravitational waves

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:20.640
this morning when you got out of bed,

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.240
just really wimpy ones.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:24.880
Even two black holes orbiting around each other

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:27.080
produce gravitational waves that are too weak

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.080
for us to detect here on Earth.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.640
But when big things like black holes

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.940
or neutron stars, slam into each other

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:37.580
they produce a veritable earthquake in spacetime.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.060
Scientists have tried to detect waves from

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:44.080
such explosive collisions for decades,

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:45.820
but picking up the signal

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:48.600
is like trying to measure a one millimeter change

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.500
in a stick that the thousand light-years long.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:56.140
The recently upgraded LIGO has finally succeeded.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:59.260
LIGO splits a laser beam between two

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.940
four-kilometer-long perpendicular tubes.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.260
The beams bounce off mirrors hundreds of times.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.840
When the beams recombine, they cancel each other out,

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:12.000
but when gravitational waves from merging black holes

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.080
hit Earth, they ever so slightly squeeze one tube

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:17.900
and stretch the other,

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:19.860
which causes the beams to wobble

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.620
in and out of alignment.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:26.000
That signal crescendos as the black holes merge.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.240
If you converted the signal to a sound,

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:30.810
this is what you would hear:

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:32.270
[chirp]

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:34.730
Evidence of gravitational waves not only

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:36.590
means that Einstein was right,

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:38.990
it also allows us to observe the universe

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:40.850
in a completely new way.

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:43.490
Right now, we peer out into the cosmos

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:45.490
through telescopes the record light

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:48.220
or other forms of electromagnetic radiation.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:50.220
Detecting gravitational waves means

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:54.000
we can study space by listening to gravity itself.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:03:05.080
[music]

